.TH GPIO-EVENT-MON 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
gpio\-event\-mon \- monitor edge events on GPIO lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \%gpio\-event\-mon
.B \-n
.I name
.B \-o
.I offset
.RB [ \-o
.IR offset ]...
.RB [ \-b
.IR period ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.br
.B \%gpio\-event\-mon \-?
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \%gpio\-event\-mon
requests one or more lines of a GPIO chip as inputs with edge detection
enabled, using the version 2 GPIO character device interface,
and prints each edge event the kernel reports for them.
.PP
The lines are requested under the consumer label
.BR \%gpio\-event\-mon .
All lines share the same configuration.
Before waiting for events, the program reads and prints the current value
of each line:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
Monitoring line 4 on gpiochip0
Initial line value: 1
.EE
.in
.PP
It then prints one line per event:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
GPIO EVENT at \fItimestamp\fP on line \fIoffset\fP (\fIline_seqno\fP|\fIseqno\fP) \fIedge\fP
.EE
.in
.PP
.I timestamp
is the event time in nanoseconds, taken from
.B CLOCK_MONOTONIC
unless
.B \-w
or
.B \-t
is given.
.I line_seqno
is the sequence number of the event among the events on that line, and
.I seqno
its sequence number among all events of the request.
.I edge
is
.BR "rising edge" ,
.BR "falling edge" ,
or
.B unknown event
for an event identifier the program does not recognise.
.PP
If neither
.B \-r
nor
.B \-f
is given, the program prints
\(dqNo flags specified, listening on both rising and falling edges\(dq
and monitors both edges.
.PP
The program runs until it has reported
.I count
events (see
.BR \-c ),
until a read from the kernel fails, or until it is killed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
Monitor lines of the GPIO character device
.RI /dev/ name ,
for example
.BR gpiochip0 .
The name is given without the
.I /dev/
prefix.
This option is required.
.TP
.BI \-o " offset"
Monitor the line at
.I offset
on the chip, given in decimal.
May be repeated to monitor several lines in one request,
up to a maximum of 64.
At least one
.B \-o
option is required.
.TP
.BI \-b " period"
Set a debounce period of
.I period
microseconds on all monitored lines.
A period of 0, the default, requests no debouncing.
.TP
.BI \-c " count"
Exit after
.I count
events have been reported.
If this option is omitted or
.I count
is 0, the program runs until it is killed.
.TP
.B \-d
Request the lines with the open-drain drive flag.
See NOTES.
.TP
.B \-s
Request the lines with the open-source drive flag.
See NOTES.
.TP
.B \-r
Report rising edges.
.TP
.B \-f
Report falling edges.
.TP
.B \-w
Timestamp events with
.B CLOCK_REALTIME
(wall-clock time) instead of
.BR CLOCK_MONOTONIC .
.TP
.B \-t
Timestamp events using the hardware timestamp engine (HTE).
See NOTES.
.TP
.B \-?
Print a usage message to standard error and exit.
An unknown option or a missing option argument has the same effect.
.PP
The
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-o ,
and
.B \-b
arguments are converted with
.BR strtoul (3)
without error checking: trailing characters are ignored and a value that does
not begin with a digit is taken as 0.
.SH FILES
.TP
.RI /dev/ name
GPIO character device named by
.BR \-n ,
opened read-only;
typically
.IR /dev/gpiochip0 ,
.IR /dev/gpiochip1 ,
and so on.
.SH NOTES
The kernel must provide the GPIO character device
.RB \%( CONFIG_GPIO_CDEV ).
The caller needs read access to the device node.
A line that is already requested by another consumer cannot be requested
again, and the program exits with an error.
.PP
The kernel accepts the open-drain and open-source drive flags only for lines
requested as outputs.
.B \%gpio\-event\-mon
always requests its lines as inputs, so a request made with
.B \-d
or
.B \-s
is rejected by the kernel with
.BR EINVAL .
.PP
Hardware timestamps
.RB ( \-t )
require a kernel built with
.B \%CONFIG_HTE
and a timestamp provider for the line; otherwise the request fails.
.B \-t
cannot be combined with
.BR \-w .
.PP
If the hardware does not debounce the line itself,
the kernel emulates debouncing in software;
an edge is then reported once the line has been stable for the debounce
period, and the event timestamp corresponds to the end of that period.
.PP
If events arrive faster than they are read, the kernel event buffer
may overflow and discard the oldest events;
gaps in the sequence numbers show where this happened.
.SH EXAMPLES
Monitor both edges on line 4 of
.I /dev/gpiochip0
with a 10 millisecond debounce period:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
gpio\-event\-mon \-n gpiochip0 \-o 4 \-r \-f \-b 10000
.EE
.in
.PP
Report the first five rising edges on lines 2 and 3:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
gpio\-event\-mon \-n gpiochip0 \-o 2 \-o 3 \-r \-c 5
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR \%gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer (8),
.BR \%gpio\-watch (8)
.PP
The GPIO character device interface and the line request flags are
described in the Linux kernel source in
.PP
.I \%Documentation/userspace\-api/gpio/chardev.rst
.br
.I \%Documentation/userspace\-api/gpio/gpio\-v2\-get\-line\-ioctl.rst
